{"document":1,"title":"user in #7527f7e2:answer_7cb94507: I'm trying to get a better handle on my finances a...","updated_at":"2023-05-23T08:26:00+00:00","source_type":"slack","content":"You're taking control of your finances and making conscious choices about where you want to allocate your money! That's fantastic! Meal planning and grocery shopping on a budget can be a game-changer for your finances and your health. Here are some suggestions to get you started: **Meal Planning:** 1. **Plan your meals for the week**: Take some time on the weekend to plan out your meals for the week. This will help you avoid last-minute takeout or dining out. 2. **Choose affordable proteins**: Opt for affordable protein sources like beans, lentils, or chicken breasts instead of expensive meats like steak or seafood. 3. **Cook in bulk**: Cooking meals in bulk can save you time and money. Consider making a big pot of soup or stew that can be reheated throughout the week. 4. **Use a meal planning app**: Apps like Plan to Eat or Yummly can help you plan meals, make grocery lists, and keep track of your nutritional intake. **Grocery Shopping:** 1. **Make a grocery list**: Stick to your list when you're at the store to avoid impulse buys. 2. **Shop sales**: Check the weekly ads for your local grocery stores and plan your meals around the items that are on sale. 3. **Buy in bulk**: Purchasing items like rice, pasta, and canned goods in bulk can save you money in the long run. 4. **Shop at discount stores**: Consider shopping at discount stores or Aldi for affordable staples like produce, meat, and dairy products. 5. **Use cashback apps**: Apps like Ibotta and Fetch Rewards offer cashback on certain grocery items. 6. **Avoid processed and pre-packaged foods**: Processed and pre-packaged foods tend to be more expensive and less healthy. Opt for whole ingredients and cook from scratch instead. **Budgeting Tips:** 1. **Set a grocery budget**: Determine how much you want to spend on groceries each week and stick to it.\n2. **Use the 50/30/20 rule**: Allocate 50% of your grocery budget to staples like produce, meat, and dairy, 30% to pantry items, and 20% to treats and snacks. 3. **Shop with a budgeting app**: Apps like Out of Milk or Grocery IQ can help you track your grocery spending and stay within your budget. Remember, small changes can add up over time. By meal planning and grocery shopping on a budget, you can save money, eat healthier, and feel more in control of your finances. How do you feel about trying out some new recipes and cooking techniques? Do you have any dietary restrictions or preferences that might impact your meal planning and grocery shopping?","metadata":"{\"Channel\": \"7527f7e2:answer_7cb94507\"}"}
