{"document":2,"title":"user in #06878be2:sharegpt_GSC090N_6: This is what happened with npm install --force. Sh...","updated_at":"2023-05-27T11:49:00+00:00","source_type":"slack","content":"Based on the error message you provided, it seems like there is a conflict with the dependency resolution. Using the `--force` flag may not be a good idea since it can override some safety checks and install dependencies with incompatible versions that can break your project.

Instead, you can try using the `--legacy-peer-deps` flag, which instructs npm to install packages even if they have peer dependencies that are not compatible with your current setup. This can sometimes work as a workaround when dealing with conflicting peer dependencies.

So you can try running the following command:
```css
npm install --legacy-peer-deps
```
This should allow npm to install packages without the conflict errors you have been encountering. However, keep in mind that this might not fix the issue entirely, as the dependencies installed may not work properly together. It is always better to use compatible versions of packages whenever possible to ensure the stability of your project.","metadata":"{"Channel": "06878be2:sharegpt_GSC090N_6"}"}
